# Doctor branch lock and temporary cover

`DOCTOR-ACCESS-PR-B-BRANCH-LOCK-COVER`, the second of three children of
`DOCTOR-ACCESS-SINGLE-SESSION-BRANCH-LOCK-1`. Full rules: `DBL-R001..R025` in
`docs/architecture/doctor-branch-lock-and-cover.md`. Operations:
`docs/runbooks/doctor-branch-lock-operations.md`.

Flag `doctor.branch_lock`, risk `critical`, committed **OFF**. It does not resolve unless
`doctor.single_active_session` is also on — see the dependency below.

## The one sentence

**THE SERVER DECIDES, THE DOCTOR NEVER DOES, AND NOBODY GUESSES.**

## Durable contract

- **UNSET is a first-class answer, not missing data.** No lock row means "behave exactly as
  this system did before this capability existed". **There is no backfill migration and none
  may be added.** Never infer a branch from the doctor code, the tablet's branch, the last
  login, a room or history — a recent branch may be *displayed* as a non-authoritative
  suggestion and never auto-written.
- **Null is an answer, never an error.** A null user, a non-doctor, an exempt account, an
  unlinked doctor, an UNSET doctor and a degraded lock all resolve to null, and every one
  means do not narrow.
- **The branch lock arms only alongside the session lease and an observable session store.**
  Enforced in `enabled()`, not by the flag registry: nothing reads a flag's `dependencies`
  array, so that declaration is decorative. An armed lock over a disarmed lease engine would
  leave an expired cover granting authority with nothing left to invalidate.
- **There is exactly ONE resolver and it is pure.** `DoctorEffectiveBranchResolver` takes a
  `User` and nothing else — no request, no device id, no branch argument — issues at most
  three bounded reads and performs **zero writes**. It must never audit: it runs on every
  protected request, so a row per page view. The reason travels on the returned value object.
  A second implementation of "is a cover active" is the worst available outcome, which is why
  the SQL predicate lives in one repository method and the PHP predicate on one model, pinned
  equal by test.
- **HOME_LOCKED_BRANCH and TEMPORARY_BRANCH_COVER are two concepts.** A cover never rewrites
  `mst_doctor_branch_locks`; the home branch is retained and returned to. Anything that wrote
  the cover target into the lock would destroy what the doctor reverts to.
- **Authority is derived from current timestamps on every request.** No `is_active` column,
  no `EXPIRED` status, no `expireStale()`, no scheduler in the correctness path. A late
  housekeeping job can leave a row uncollected; it can never leave an expired cover in force.
- **Cover periods are half-open `[starts_at, ends_at)`, so ADJACENCY IS ALLOWED.** 08:00–12:00
  then 12:00–16:00 do not overlap. Prove the acceptance, not only the refusal — an over-strict
  comparison passes every refusal test and then rejects the most ordinary pair of covers an
  approver files. Overlap is **two** comparisons; a test filed in chronological order pins
  only one of them, so file a reverse-order pair as well.
- **Non-overlap is per DOCTOR.** Two doctors may hold covers over the same instants, including
  for the same target branch.
- **No index can express overlap** — a unique index compares values, overlap is a range
  predicate; measured, not assumed. The invariant rests on
  `DoctorBranchCoverApprovalService::approve()` in this order: lock the cover row, lock the
  doctor row, **lock the home-lock row (the serialisation point)**, then evaluate overlap,
  then write. Evaluating earlier lets two approvers each read clean and each commit.
- **A cover requires a home lock.** `source_home_branch_id` is NOT NULL, an UNSET doctor has
  no row to serialise on, and expiry would otherwise silently widen an UNSET doctor back to
  every RME branch — a widening on a timer.
- **A permanent transfer is refused while a cover is CURRENT.** A future-dated cover does not
  block and is surfaced to the approver.
- **Maker-checker is an ACTOR rule, enforced in the service inside the transaction.**
  `request.created_by` may never equal the approving actor. Do not encode "Super Admin is the
  maker" or "Supervisor RME is the checker" — either tier may be either party. The check
  cannot live in a policy: the single global `Gate::before` returns true for a Super Admin
  before any policy method runs. A requester who later gains or changes role still cannot
  approve their own request.
- **A rejection requires a written reason**; an approval note stays optional. Enforced in the
  service so an HTTP caller and a console caller cannot diverge; the FormRequest mirrors it
  only for a better message.
- **An approval is an event, not a credential.** The lock is written inside the approval
  transaction; there is no token to replay at a second endpoint, because there is no second
  endpoint.
- **Every binding is re-derived under the lock at decision time**: request type from the live
  lock, a transfer's source still where the request said, destination still active and RME,
  target inside the doctor's practice branches, presence read live.
- **There is no day boundary and no EXPIRED status on a lock request.** The sibling
  `trx_branch_change_requests` has one because that row belongs to a clinical date. A
  permanent lock has no day; its freshness boundary is the stale-source guard. Do not
  "restore" a state that was never missing.
- **An approved change invalidates the session; a branch never switches inside an open one.**
  Activation, expiry and transfer are one event: the value recorded on the lease at claim time
  no longer matches the value derived now. The deny arm fires only when **both** sides have an
  answer, so disarming logs nobody out and arming mass-evicts nobody.
- **A branch operation never revokes an identity.** Device, `DoctorDeviceAuthorization` and
  WebAuthn credential all survive an approval, a rejection and an expiry. **Identity
  revocation is not a branch rollback**; the undo is a transfer back.
- **Two hooks, and narrowing is an INTERSECTION.** `RmeWorkingBranchScope::resolve()` (the
  list scope) and `ClinicVisitService::resolveBranchId()` (the write chokepoint), each asking
  the resolver once. `array_intersect` makes the hook structurally incapable of granting a
  branch the pre-sprint rules withheld; a locked branch that is not an active RME branch
  yields an **empty** set, not an unreadable one.
- **Per-record reads stay cross-branch on purpose.** `RmeWorkingBranchScope::allows()`,
  `ClinicVisitPolicy` and `DoctorClinicalBranchResolver` are NOT narrowed — a patient's record
  book anchors on their earliest visit, so narrowing would hide a patient's history from the
  doctor treating them. Do not "align" them with the list scope; the docblocks say so.
- **The tablet's branch and the request's `branch_id` decide nothing.** Both the
  online-context start and visit creation re-assert server-side; a crafted value is refused at
  the service boundary, not merely hidden in the view.
- **A degraded lock must be VISIBLE.** A locked branch that loses `is_active` or
  `is_rme_enabled` degrades to UNSET, which is correct and not the same as visible. The
  approver queue lists it with the retained home branch, the reason **code** and a sentence,
  and puts each row to the resolver rather than re-implementing the predicate.

## Two traps that are silent in both directions

- **Instants must cross the SQL boundary in the frame the COLUMNS store.**
  `Connection::prepareBindings()` formats a `DateTimeInterface` using the timezone the object
  carries. Hand a cover predicate a clinical-zone (WITA) reading of the right instant and the
  binding lands eight hours from the column, so `starts_at <= at < ends_at` matches nothing,
  the caller sees "no active cover" and every guard built on it returns normally. That is
  exactly how the transfer-through-active-cover guard came to be dead code. Normalise at the
  one repository boundary; it converts a frame, never an instant.
- **Adding a foreign-key column re-creates a partial index WITHOUT its predicate.** SQLite
  rebuilds the table and Laravel re-creates indexes from introspection, flattening
  `UNIQUE(user_id) WHERE released_at IS NULL` into a plain unique on `user_id` — which breaks
  every doctor's second login after any logout, **on SQLite only, invisible to a green
  PostgreSQL gate**. Re-assert the index with its predicate in both `up()` and `down()`, and
  run the migration BOTH ways.

## Test traps that make a green test assert nothing

- `daArmDoctorAccess()` arms **two** flags and the session driver. Arming `doctor.branch_lock`
  alone leaves the resolver disabled and every answer null, so the test proves legacy
  behaviour while claiming to prove the lock.
- `firstOrNew(['doctor_id' => …])` mass-assigns its search key through the constructor and
  these models have an empty `$fillable` by design, so it throws before `forceFill()`. Look
  the row up, then build a bare model. Measured as 57 of 95 failures in one run.
- `status` and the decision stamps are not fillable, so a cover built with `create()` alone
  stays PENDING — the resolver then returns the home branch and the test silently proves
  nothing.
- Cover fixtures are INSTANT pairs, never clinical dates. Operator input parses in WITA while
  the app timezone is UTC, so build operator strings from `ClinicalClock::now()` and directly
  written rows from `now()`.
- A boundary test must share ONE instant between the two rows. Deriving the two ends from two
  separate `now()` reads makes a "shared" boundary two instants apart, and the case then
  passes under either comparison operator.
- Concurrency is not provable locally: `lockForUpdate` compiles to an empty string on SQLite.
  Only the PostgreSQL critical gate exercises it, which is why `DoctorAccess` is a required
  critical-gate filter token.

## Scope boundary

The bulk device-authorization command is **PR-C**. Global doctor WebAuthn enforcement stays
**false** and this capability never reads its flag. The parent GO tag
`doctor-access-single-session-branch-lock-1-go` is created by no child.
